---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/iran-war/">Iran war cost</a><span>September 15, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Congressional Budget Office &middot; September 15, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>On September 15 the Congressional Budget Office sent Rep. Brendan Boyle a 19-page letter estimating that the war with Iran added ${table1.total} billion to the Defense Department's costs through August 1. Most of that is the price of replacing munitions already fired. The estimate measures added costs to the Defense Department.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO focused on the incremental costs of the conflict for" /></li>
          <li>The Defense Department did not answer CBO's requests for information, so CBO built the estimate from public reports and government databases. The Associated Press reported on September 18 that U.S. Central Command's own count was ${centcom.total} billion as of September 3.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="DoD did not respond to CBO’s requests for information" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="$43.6 billion as of Sept. 3" /></li>
          <li>Higher energy prices from the war push inflation {inflation.pce2027q1Pp} percentage points above CBO's February forecast by early 2027. CBO says the United States has probably used between one-half and two-thirds of its interceptor stock since June 2025.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="0.5 percentage points higher" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="between one-half and two-thirds" /> Rebuilding that stock, CBO says, would probably take at least five years.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="at least five years" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on CBO letter 62756 of September 15, 2026, to Rep. Brendan Boyle; Boyle's March 5 request letter and his March 5, June 24, and September 15 statements; the White House's March 12 Operation Epic Fury release; OMB Director Russell Vought's June 24 supplemental request; the Lead Inspector General report on Operation Epic Fury covering April 1 to June 30, 2026; the Congressional Research Service report on the War Powers Resolution; and coverage checked against those records. CBO's site does not let its pages be saved by an ordinary fetch. The letter PDF and the publication page used here are Wayback Machine snapshots from September 17 and September 16. The Washington Post's September 18 report on the Central Command estimate served only a subscription stub; the Associated Press dispatch on the same document is the record used.</p>
        <p class="sources-line">Operation Epic Fury follows CBO's use for the whole conflict, and Department of War appears as Defense Department outside direct quotations.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The letter, and what the $38.1 billion counts</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 15, 2026, CBO Director Phillip Swagel signed a letter to Rep. Brendan Boyle, the top Democrat on the House Budget Committee, titled "Estimating the Cost of Combat Operations Against Iran." Boyle had asked for it on March 5, {computed.daysMar5ToSep15} days earlier and five days after U.S. forces began striking Iran at 1:15 am Eastern on February 28. The answer is ${table1.total} billion through August 1. That is CBO's estimate of the costs the fighting added on top of what the Defense Department would have spent anyway: mostly the price of replacing munitions already fired, plus flying hours above peacetime, fuel, lost equipment, and other operations. It is not a count of money paid out. The department's inspector general counts ${ig.obligations} billion committed to the operation by June 30 and ${ig.disbursed} billion of that paid out.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Estimating the Cost of Combat Operations Against Iran" /><Cite s="centcom-epic-fury-2026-02-28" passage="began striking targets at 1:15 am ET" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO focused on the incremental costs of the conflict for" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="disbursed $4.9 billion or 65 percent of obligated" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO built the figure from government databases and public reports because, it says twice, the Defense Department did not respond to its requests for information, which leaves the estimates "subject to considerable uncertainty." On September 18 the Associated Press reported U.S. Central Command's own count, a one-page breakdown to lawmakers: ${centcom.total} billion as of September 3. The letter calls the whole conflict Operation Epic Fury, a name the department used for the first phase, from February 28 to the April 8 ceasefire, and later dropped.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="subject to considerable uncertainty" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="one-page breakdown to Congress this week" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="this letter refers to the entire conflict as Operation Epic Fury" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The letter also measures what the war used up. Comparing reported interceptor firings with what the department has bought, CBO says the United States "has probably used between one-half and two-thirds of its inventory" of missile-defense interceptors since June 2025, and that rebuilding it would probably take at least five years even with more production.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="between one-half and two-thirds" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="at least five years" /> The shortfall, CBO writes, would become especially problematic against an opponent with many ballistic and cruise missiles, and CBO names China as maintaining such an arsenal, which would probably play a major role in a fight over Taiwan.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="especially problematic" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="role in a military conflict involving Taiwan" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Munitions are the largest piece: ${munitions.total} billion, {computed.munitionsSharePct} percent of the total. Of that, ${munitions.interceptors} billion is missile-defense interceptors (Patriot, THAAD, SM-3, and SM-6), ${munitions.cruise} billion is land-attack cruise missiles (Tomahawks and JASSM), and ${munitions.other} billion is other munitions; CBO names the Center for Strategic and International Studies as its most detailed public source for those counts. Extra flying hours are ${table1.flyingHours} billion, fuel ${table1.fuel} billion, equipment lost in battle ${table1.equipment} billion, and other operations, including hostile fire pay and combat-zone tax exclusions, ${table1.otherOps} billion. CBO could not put a number on repairing damaged bases. Behind the bill is the size of the force: about {force.tacticalAircraft} tactical aircraft, {force.carrierStrikeGroups} aircraft-carrier strike groups, and an amphibious ready group took part in the first phase, backed by bombers, airlifters, tankers, more Navy ships, and Army air-defense, aviation, and artillery units, which CBO calls smaller and briefer than Desert Storm or Iraqi Freedom and larger than most limited air campaigns since the 1999 campaign against Yugoslavia.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$21.7 billion: $7.3 billion for the land-attack cruise" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="The most detailed publicly available information was provided by the Center for Strategic and" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="hostile fire pay, combat zone" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO is unable to estimate the cost" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Approximately 250 tactical aircraft" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="than most limited air campaigns conducted since Operation Allied Force" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>What CBO counts in the $38.1 billion</strong><span>Billions of dollars through August 1. Base repairs are not estimated.</span></figcaption>
          <BarChart bars={costBars} unit="b" ariaLabel="CBO cost categories for the Iran war in billions of dollars" />
          <p class="chart-source">Munitions alone outweigh the other four lines together.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$21.7 billion: $7.3 billion for the land-attack cruise" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">CBO letter 62756, Table 1, recovered from an Internet Archive copy of the PDF saved September 17, 2026. The five printed lines add to {computed.table1RoundedSum} before CBO's rounding note.</p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">If the fighting stays as quiet as May and June, CBO says a further month costs ${monthly.low} billion. A month like July costs ${monthly.july} billion, and more if the violence escalates.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="an additional month of conflict would cost $2 billion" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Boyle had asked four things: the department's operating and supply costs; other costs such as diplomacy and foreign aid; what the war crowds out, including moving an aircraft carrier away from Taiwan; and the effect on prices. CBO answered each separately and warned that the four are "not directly comparable or summable." His March 5 letter also said the war began without first notifying Congress or seeking authorization under the Constitution or the War Powers Act.<Cite s="boyle-letter-to-cbo-2026-03-05" passage="The operational, logistical, and sustainment costs of the war in Iran" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="not directly comparable or summable" /><Cite s="boyle-letter-to-cbo-2026-03-05" passage="without first notifying Congress or seeking authorization under the Constitution or the War Powers Act" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Boyle's statement the same day put the total at nearly $40 billion.<Cite s="boyle-cbo-findings-2026-09-15" passage="nearly $40 billion through August 1, 2026" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Brendan Boyle, House Budget ranking member" when="September 15, 2026" where="House Budget Committee Democrats statement">
          <p>this nonpartisan CBO report makes clear that the war has also cost American taxpayers tens of billions of dollars and counting, while continuing to drive up costs<Cite s="boyle-cbo-findings-2026-09-15" passage="tens of billions of dollars and counting" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">${table1.total} billion is the added cost through August 1, estimated without the department's answers, with the base repairs still uncounted.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="as of August 1, 2026, the armed conflict with Iran has cost DoD" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="DoD did not respond to CBO’s requests for information" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO is unable to estimate the cost" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="cbo-62756-iran" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Table 1 categories, billions of dollars through August 1: munitions {table1.munitions}, equipment {table1.equipment}, flying hours {table1.flyingHours}, other operations {table1.otherOps}, fuel {table1.fuel}, bases not available, total {table1.total}. CBO notes that numbers may not add to the total because of rounding.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="cbo-62756-iran" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Defense Department gave the name Operation Epic Fury to the first phase of the conflict, February 28 to the April 8 ceasefire, kept using it for a time after the ceasefire, and then stopped. CBO's footnote says, "To minimize confusion, this letter refers to the entire conflict as Operation Epic Fury." The United States has enforced a blockade of Iranian commercial shipping. CBO dates the president's declaration that the ceasefire was over to July 10, after Iranian attacks on tankers. The inspector general writes that on July 8 the president declared the MoU over. The two records do not explain the gap.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Operation Epic Fury is the name that the Department of Defense gave to the initial, intense phase" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="this letter refers to the entire conflict as Operation Epic Fury" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="has enforced a blockade of Iranian commercial shipping" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="the President declared that the ceasefire was over" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="On July 8, President Trump" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="declared the MoU was" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="wh-epic-fury-2026-03-12" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The White House's March 12 release stated the campaign's aims as obliterating Iran's ballistic missile arsenal and production capacity, annihilating its navy, severing support for terrorist proxies, and ensuring Iran never acquires a nuclear weapon.<Cite s="wh-epic-fury-2026-03-12" passage="obliterate Iran’s ballistic missile arsenal and production capacity" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>CBO asked the Defense Department for its numbers and got none. On September 18 the Associated Press reported Central Command's own count.</h3>
              CBO's letter says the department did not respond to its requests for information. On September 18 the Associated Press reported Central Command's one-page breakdown putting the war at ${centcom.total} billion as of September 3, with munitions at ${centcom.munitions} billion against CBO's ${munitions.total} billion, and base damage still left out. The counts are {computed.daysAug1ToSep3} days apart and use different lists of costs. CBO published without the department's numbers; the Associated Press reported Central Command's count on September 18.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="DoD did not respond to CBO’s requests for information" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="$43.6 billion as of Sept. 3" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="$28.1 billion in U.S. Central Command’s estimate" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="does not include damage to U.S. bases in the Middle East" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Hegseth's $37.5 billion covered the war to date plus the rest of the fiscal year.</h3>
              On July 21 Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth told the Senate Appropriations Committee the war had cost ${admin.hegseth} billion. The committee's caption track has him giving that figure as the estimate as of that day, and saying some of it is anticipated through the fiscal year, through September 30. Military Times reported the figure as the cost to date. Stars and Stripes, from the same hearing, reported that the figure was "based on where the conflict is today and projecting costs until the end of the fiscal year on Sept. 30." CBO's letter also puts that ${admin.hegseth} billion through September 2026. The committee hearing page dates the appearance to July 21; CBO dates it to July 22 and labels it Senate Armed Services testimony. A figure that includes the rest of the fiscal year's expected spending counts a different period from CBO's ${table1.total} billion through August 1.<Cite s="sac-hearing-2026-07-21" passage="Tuesday, July 21, 2026" /><Cite s="senate-approps-vtt-00479" passage="THE ESTIMATE WE HAVE AS OF TODAY'S $37.5 BILLION" /><Cite s="senate-approps-vtt-00481" passage="WE ANTICIPATE THROUGH THE FISCAL YEAR" /><Cite s="senate-approps-vtt-00482" passage="THROUGH SEPTEMBER 30" /><Cite s="militarytimes-hegseth-2026-07-21" passage="$37.5 billion to date" /><Cite s="stripes-hegseth-2026-07-21" passage="based on where the conflict is today and projecting costs until the end of the fiscal year on Sept. 30" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="through September 2026 would be" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="On July 22, 2026, in testimony about that request" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>The June request includes costs outside the war estimate.</h3>
              On June 24 the White House asked Congress for ${admin.supplementalTotal} billion. CBO says ${admin.dodRequest} billion of that is for the Defense Department, including $21 billion for munitions and ${admin.operationalRequest} billion for operational costs, and that ${admin.directRelated} billion looks directly related to the conflict, ${computed.directMinusCbo} billion above its own estimate. The rest includes ${admin.agriculture} billion for Agriculture, classified programs CBO did not analyze, and items CBO says are not a direct result of the fighting, among them $1 billion for Penn Station. Why it matters: the request is money asked for, not money spent, and Congress has not approved it.<Cite s="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" passage="a request for $87.6 billion in" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$17.3 billion for “operational costs.”" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$42.3 billion" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$11.1 billion for the Department of Agriculture" /><Cite s="ap-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="but Congress has not approved it" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="catch-minor">Smaller slips: CBO's opening summary says "approximately $38 billion" where its table prints ${table1.total} billion; Boyle's statement says nearly $40 billion; ABC put CBO's August 1 cutoff at the end of July; CBS's headline uses the $2 billion low end of CBO's $2 billion to $3 billion monthly range.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="approximately $38 billion" /><Cite s="boyle-cbo-findings-2026-09-15" passage="nearly $40 billion through August 1, 2026" /><Cite s="abc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="through the end of July" /><Cite s="cbs-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="$2 billion to $3 billion per month" /></p>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Six official totals for one war</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the first strikes to the Central Command count</strong><span>Dated steps from Central Command, Boyle's letter, the White House request, the House and Senate, CBO, the inspector general, and AP.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} />
          <p class="chart-source">CBO's cutoff is August 1, {computed.daysFeb28ToAug1} days after February 28. Central Command's cutoff, September 3, is {computed.daysAug1ToSep3} days later still.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="as of August 1, 2026, the armed conflict with Iran has cost DoD" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="$43.6 billion as of Sept. 3" /></p>
        </figure>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The same war, six published totals</strong><span>Billions of dollars. Each row is a different date and a different list of what counts.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Whose figure, and through when", "Amount", "What it counts"]} rows={clocksRows} highlightColumn={1} />
          <p class="chart-source">Hegseth's ${admin.hegseth} billion looks through September 30, after Central Command's September 3 count, so the published totals do not rise with their cutoff dates. CBO, the inspector general, and Central Command leave base damage out of their totals. CBO's ${table1.equipment} billion for equipment lost in battle counts only the losses that would change the department's replacement plans; if the department sought extra appropriations for all its battle losses, CBO says the total could be ${battleLosses.alternativeBillion} billion instead of ${table1.equipment} billion.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="through September 2026 would be" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="$43.6 billion as of Sept. 3" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="but does not include costs" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO is unable to estimate the cost" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="does not include damage to U.S. bases in the Middle East" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="could increase to $3.3 billion instead of the $1.9 billion" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">None of this money was set aside for the war. The inspector general writes that Congress did not appropriate funds specifically for the operation in fiscal 2026 and that the department's components paid for it from base-budget funds that would otherwise have gone to planned training and maintenance. On July 22 the House passed, {votes.hconres113HouseYea} to {votes.hconres113HouseNay}, the budget resolution for fiscal 2027, which the House Budget Committee chairman presented as advancing reconciliation, a budget procedure, to support the troops. The resolution tells four committees to submit changes in law that would raise the deficit by as much as ${votes.hconres113ArmedServices} billion at Armed Services, ${votes.hconres113Intelligence} billion at Intelligence, ${votes.hconres113Agriculture} billion at Agriculture, and ${votes.hconres113HouseAdmin} billion at House Administration, ${votes.hconres113Total} billion in all. Those instructions are a budget blueprint, separate from the administration's ${admin.supplementalTotal} billion request.<Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="Congress did not appropriate funds specifically for OEF" /><Cite s="house-budget-hconres-113-2026-07-22" passage="passed by a vote of 216-214 H. Con. Res. 113" /><Cite s="hconres-113-eh-2026-07-22" passage="not more than $60,000,000,000" /><Cite s="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" passage="a request for $87.6 billion in" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Each published total has its own cutoff and list of costs. The request rows are money Congress has been asked for but has not approved.<Cite s="ap-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="but Congress has not approved it" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" kind="record" detail>
          <p>OMB Director Russell T. Vought wrote Speaker Mike Johnson on June 24 transmitting "a request for $87.6 billion in supplemental funding," most of it for Operation Epic Fury plus other needs including Ebola response and support for farmers. The enclosed table assigns ${admin.dodRequest} billion to the entity it calls the Department of War.<Cite s="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" passage="a request for $87.6 billion in" /><Cite s="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" passage="$67.1 billion for the Department of War" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Prices at home, the stockpile, and the people in the fight</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO's price path starts with oil. Brent crude, in CBO's telling, went from ${inflation.brentQ4_2025} a barrel in the last quarter of 2025 to a peak of ${inflation.brentQ2_2026} in the second quarter of 2026, and CBO's updated path, using information through August 17, has it at ${inflation.brentEnd2026} by the end of 2026 and ${inflation.brentEnd2027} by the end of 2027, still {inflation.brentThisYearPctHigher} percent above its February forecast this year. Gasoline, diesel, and jet fuel moved first; by early 2027, CBO says, roughly {inflation.fuelShareOfLevelChangePct} percent of the price change from the war is fuel and the rest is other goods and services. That is why CBO puts year-over-year inflation, measured by the PCE index, the price index for what households buy, {inflation.pce2027q1Pp} percentage points above its February forecast in early 2027; in the second quarter of 2026 alone, energy added {inflation.q2EnergyAddPp} points to an annualized inflation rate of {inflation.q2PcePct} percent. Natural gas is the exception: CBO says U.S. prices "have not increased significantly, because of domestic production."<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Brent crude prices rose from $64 a barrel" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="roughly 40 percent of the total change" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="0.5 percentage points higher" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="added 2.3 percentage points" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="have not increased significantly, because of domestic production" /></p>
        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>the rest of CBO's price figures</summary>
          <p>By the first quarter of 2027 the PCE price level is {inflation.pceLevel2027q1Pct} percent above the February forecast; the year-over-year rate is {inflation.pce2027q1Pp} points higher. Core PCE, which leaves out food and energy, is {inflation.corePce2027q1Pp} points higher on both. Three-month Treasury bill rates are nearly {inflation.tbill2026Pp} points higher in 2026. CBO says the effects on revenues and spending over 2026 to 2036 are modest and roughly offsetting, and that it did not fold in effects on real output.</p>
        </details>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Inside the department, higher fuel prices arrive through the Defense Logistics Agency, which buys fuel for it. CBO says the agency added a ${fuel.surchargePerBarrel}-a-barrel surcharge from April to June, ${fuel.surchargeMillion} million more for the services, and that in June the Government Accountability Office expected the agency to lose ${fuel.dlaLossBillion} billion on fuel sales in fiscal 2026 even so. Those two figures together are CBO's ${table1.fuel} billion fuel line.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="the Government Accountability Office" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="a loss of $1,822 million in fiscal year" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO also prices the interceptors themselves: it puts Patriot and SM-6 interceptors at roughly ${interceptorPrices.patriotSm6Million} million each, THAAD at about ${interceptorPrices.thaadMillion} million, and the most capable SM-3 at ${interceptorPrices.sm3Million} million, and says new orders have historically taken {interceptorPrices.orderLagYearsLow} to {interceptorPrices.orderLagYearsHigh} years to deliver. That is the shortfall the letter says would become especially problematic against an opponent with many ballistic and cruise missiles, and CBO names China as maintaining such an arsenal.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="ranging from roughly $4 million" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="especially problematic" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On the ground, the inspector general counts more than {ig.deployed.toLocaleString("en-US")} U.S. service members in the Central Command area for this operation through June 30. Its text reports {ig.kiaThroughJune30} killed in action and {ig.nonHostileThroughJune30} killed in non-hostile events from February 28 to June 30, {ig.woundedThroughJune30} wounded in action, and {ig.kiaJuly} more killed in action in July; its table of deaths totals {ig.table4Total} as of August 26, {ig.table4Kia} killed in action and {ig.table4NonHostile} non-hostile, and places the seventh non-hostile death on July 1, one day past the text's window. CBO's letter assigns no dollar figure to those deaths and injuries, and says so; Boyle's March 5 statement had named "the tragic deaths of American service members." The State Department told the inspectors that damage to its embassies and facilities in Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, and the United Arab Emirates was about ${ig.diplomaticMillion} million as of June 30, and that it had incurred ${ig.stateTotalMillion} million on the conflict as of June 2, ${ig.stateContingencyMillion} million of it on evacuations and related contingencies. The bases those troops live on carry no official figure: CBO could not estimate repairs because the department did not provide values and host nations might pay part of any rebuild. An independent model from the Center for Strategic and International Studies puts damage to bases and fixed infrastructure at ${csisCost.basesLow} billion to ${csisCost.basesHigh} billion, and veterans' costs near ${csisCost.veterans30yr} billion over 30 years, with wide uncertainty on how many buildings were hit and what was inside them.<Cite s="csis-epic-fury-cost" passage="Damage to Bases and Fixed Infrastructure" /><Cite s="csis-epic-fury-cost" passage="The total cost over 30 years ($12 billion)" /><Cite s="csis-epic-fury-cost" passage="The estimate captured three uncertainties with base damage" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="More than 50,000 U.S. Service members were deployed" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="seven U.S. Service members were killed" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="Data as of August 26, 2026" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="does not assign financial costs to U.S. military personnel" /><Cite s="boyle-cbo-request-2026-03-05" passage="the tragic deaths of American service members" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="approximately $184 million" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="incurred $113 million in costs related to the" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO is unable to estimate the cost" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Farmers appear only in the June request. The White House asked for ${admin.agriculture} billion for Agriculture, including ${admin.agricultureCropAssistance} billion of temporary economic assistance for 2026 row and specialty crops, which CBO links to higher fuel and fertilizer prices after the Strait of Hormuz closed to commercial shipping. Congress has not approved that money.<Cite s="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" passage="$11.1 billion to support" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$11.1 billion for the Department of Agriculture" /><Cite s="ap-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="but Congress has not approved it" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Brown University's Costs of War brief of May 18 measured a different bill: what drivers paid for gasoline and diesel above a "no-war" estimate of prices, over ${brownEnergy.extraFuelBillion} billion, more than ${brownEnergy.householdDollars} per household. Brown counts the pump, so its figures are a separate bill from CBO's Defense Department estimate. By September 7 the same measure had passed ${brownEnergy.extraFuelBillionSep7} billion and ${brownEnergy.householdDollarsSep7} per household.<Cite s="brown-iran-war-energy-cost-2026-05-18" passage="over $40 billion on" /><Cite s="brown-iran-war-energy-cost-2026-05-18" passage="over $300 more for gasoline and diesel" /><Cite s="brown-iran-war-energy-cost-2026-05-18" passage="no-war" /><Cite s="brown-iran-war-energy-cost-2026-09-08" passage="surpassed $100 billion" /><Cite s="brown-iran-war-energy-cost-2026-09-08" passage="over $750 more for gasoline and diesel" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO focused on the incremental costs of the conflict for" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Iranian civilians are counted in a separate record. On September 17 the Associated Press reported that a fact-finding mission commissioned by the U.N. Human Rights Council found "reasonable grounds" to believe the United States committed war crimes in two strikes on February 28, including one on a school in Minab. The mission, as AP reported it, says the pair of strikes killed at least {unMission.civilians} civilians. The report is not binding. Its statement, as AP quoted it, names the crime. The same experts, as AP reported them, found that Iran's government committed crimes against humanity against its own civilians, including murder, imprisonment, and torture.<Cite s="ap-un-iran-2026-09-17" passage="reasonable grounds" /><Cite s="ap-un-iran-2026-09-17" passage="a pair of U.S. airstrikes that killed at least 178 civilians" /><Cite s="ap-un-iran-2026-09-17" passage="while not binding" /><Cite s="ap-un-iran-2026-09-17" passage="crimes against humanity against its own people" /><Cite s="ap-un-iran-2026-09-17" passage="including murder, imprisonment, torture" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="U.N. fact-finding mission on Iran, statement" when="September 17, 2026" where="As quoted by the Associated Press">
          <p>The mission found reasonable grounds to believe the United States committed the war crime of launching indiscriminate attacks resulting in the loss of life or injury to civilians or damage to civilian objects<Cite s="ap-un-iran-2026-09-17" passage="The mission found reasonable grounds to believe the United States committed the war crime of launching indiscriminate attacks" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The State Department said it does not give credibility to findings in the report, and White House spokeswoman Anna Kelly said the only party in the conflict that has committed war crimes is Iran's government.<Cite s="ap-un-iran-2026-09-17" passage="we do not give credibility to findings in this report" /><Cite s="ap-un-iran-2026-09-17" passage="The only party in this conflict that has committed war crimes is the Iranian regime" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Ten outlets on one letter and one hearing</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>
        <p class="oc-count" data-layer="fact">Ten outlets checked against CBO's letter, the inspector general's report, and the Senate hearing captions. One mislabels the inspector general's figures. One mislabels Hegseth's $37.5 billion as the cost to date. Checks out means the record says what the outlet wrote; consistent means the outlet's wording fits the record without repeating its exact number or date; mislabeled means the outlet attached a number or date to the wrong thing; unconfirmed means no record settles it.</p>
        <div data-layer="fact">
        <OutletCheck outlet="Reuters" status="warn" chip="mislabeled" claim={`wrote that the inspector general put the war cost "through June 19 at $33.4 billion" and that the amount "included about $184 million in damages to U.S. diplomatic facilities."`} verdict="Mislabeled. The inspector general's $33.4 billion is as of June 29, and it is the sum of $7.4 billion committed, $22.3 billion of munitions, and $3.7 billion of equipment losses; the $184 million is the State Department's separate figure for damage to its own buildings. Reuters's $3 billion a month is CBO's figure for a month as intense as July, and CBO also gives $2 billion for a quiet month. Its July 21 hearing date matches the committee's page.">
          <Cite s="reuters-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="through June 19 at $33.4 billion" />
          <Cite s="reuters-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="included about $184 million in damages to U.S. diplomatic facilities" />
          <Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="$33.4 billion as of June 29" />
          <Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="approximately $184 million" />
          <Cite s="reuters-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="rise by $3 billion a month" />
          <Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="an additional month of conflict would cost $2 billion" />
          <Cite s="sac-hearing-2026-07-21" passage="Tuesday, July 21, 2026" />
        </OutletCheck>
        <OutletCheck outlet="Military Times" status="warn" chip="mislabeled" claim={`wrote on July 21 that the war had cost the Defense Department "$37.5 billion to date," as Hegseth told senators.`} verdict={`Wrong on "to date." The committee's caption track has Hegseth giving $37.5 billion as the estimate as of that day, and saying some of it is anticipated through the fiscal year, through September 30.`}>
          <Cite s="militarytimes-hegseth-2026-07-21" passage="$37.5 billion to date" />
          <Cite s="senate-approps-vtt-00479" passage="THE ESTIMATE WE HAVE AS OF TODAY'S $37.5 BILLION" />
          <Cite s="senate-approps-vtt-00481" passage="WE ANTICIPATE THROUGH THE FISCAL YEAR" />
          <Cite s="senate-approps-vtt-00482" passage="THROUGH SEPTEMBER 30" />
