        </div>
        <p data-layer="narrative">NBC carried the White House's statement on the letter, from spokeswoman Anna Kelly. The statement addresses the munitions stock.<Cite s="nbc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="more than enough munitions, ammo, and stockpiles" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Anna Kelly, White House spokeswoman" when="September 15, 2026" where="As quoted by NBC News">
          <p>The United States Military has more than enough munitions, ammo, and stockpiles to serve all of the Commander-in-Chief’s strategic goals and beyond.<Cite s="nbc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="more than enough munitions, ammo, and stockpiles" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Reuters quoted the Defense Department's chief spokesman, Sean Parnell, disputing the letter's munitions findings: "We have everything required to strike at the time and place of the president’s choosing."<Cite s="reuters-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="We have everything required to strike at the time and place of the president’s choosing" /></p>
      </section>

      <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the shares and day counts were computed</summary>
          <p>Munitions share: {munitions.total} / {table1.total} = {computed.munitionsSharePct} percent. Energy share of second-quarter PCE inflation, used only to check NBC: {inflation.q2EnergyAddPp} / {inflation.q2PcePct} = {computed.q2EnergySharePct} percent. Days from February 28 to August 1: {computed.daysFeb28ToAug1} elapsed days; inclusive count 155; {table1.total} billion / 155 = ${computed.perDayInclusiveMillion} million a day. Days from August 1 to September 3: {computed.daysAug1ToSep3}. Table 1 printed parts {table1.munitions} + {table1.equipment} + {table1.flyingHours} + {table1.otherOps} + {table1.fuel} = {computed.table1RoundedSum}, against a printed total of {table1.total}. Direct-request minus CBO: {admin.directRelated} - {table1.total} = {computed.directMinusCbo}. CBO minus inspector general: {table1.total} - {ig.total} = {computed.cboMinusIg}. Central Command minus CBO: {centcom.total} - {table1.total} = {computed.centcomMinusCbo}; munitions {centcom.munitions} - {munitions.total} = {computed.centcomMunitionsMinusCbo}. Inspector general total: {ig.obligations} + {ig.munitions} + {ig.equipment} = {ig.total}. Fuel: {fuel.surchargeMillion} + {fuel.dlaLossMillion} million = {fuel.totalBillion} billion after rounding.</p>
      </details>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The money, the votes, and a larger count</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">AP reported on September 15 that Congress has not approved the June request. ABC quoted Senate Majority Leader John Thune that day saying the funding push would "probably be a post-election push."<Cite s="ap-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="but Congress has not approved it" /><Cite s="abc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="that'll probably be a post-election push" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Congress has also voted on the war itself. On June 3 the House passed, {votes.hconres86HouseYea} to {votes.hconres86HouseNay}, a concurrent resolution directing the president to remove U.S. forces from hostilities with Iran; a concurrent resolution is a measure both chambers adopt that is not sent to the president to sign or veto. The Senate agreed on June 23, {votes.hconres86SenateYea} to {votes.hconres86SenateNay}. On July 23 the House passed another, {votes.hconres89HouseYea} to {votes.hconres89HouseNay}. On September 15, the day of CBO's letter, the House passed a third, {votes.hconres93HouseYea} to {votes.hconres93HouseNay}.<Cite s="clerk-hconres-86-2026199" passage="H. Con. Res. 86" /><Cite s="crs-r42699-war-powers-2019-03-08" passage="concurrent resolution is adopted by both chambers, but it does not require presentment to the President for signature or veto" /><Cite s="senate-floor-2026-06-23" passage="Resolution agreed to in Senate without amendment by Yea-Nay Vote. 50 - 48" /><Cite s="clerk-hconres-89-2026282" passage="H. Con. Res. 89" /><Cite s="clerk-hconres-93-2026307" passage="H. Con. Res. 93" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Whether those votes can force a withdrawal is disputed. The War Powers Resolution says the president must remove forces when Congress so directs by concurrent resolution. The Congressional Research Service writes that the Supreme Court's 1983 decision in INS v. Chadha struck down a one-House legislative veto, that the decision cast doubt on veto devices not presented to the president, and that the Court later affirmed a ruling striking down a concurrent-resolution disapproval in another statute. CRS then says that War Powers provision "is constitutionally suspect under the reasoning applied by the Court." Congress later added a route through a joint resolution, which does go to the president. The votes record majorities of both chambers opposed to the hostilities; their legal force as a mechanism compelling withdrawal is disputed.<Cite s="crs-r42699-war-powers-2019-03-08" passage="Section 5(c) requires forces to be removed by the President if Congress so directs by a concurrent resolution" /><Cite s="crs-r42699-war-powers-2019-03-08" passage="ruled unconstitutional the legislative veto provision" /><Cite s="crs-r42699-war-powers-2019-03-08" passage="cast doubt on the validity of any legislative veto device" /><Cite s="crs-r42699-war-powers-2019-03-08" passage="disapprove by concurrent (two-House) resolution" /><Cite s="crs-r42699-war-powers-2019-03-08" passage="is constitutionally suspect under the reasoning applied by the Court" /><Cite s="crs-r42699-war-powers-2019-03-08" passage="substituting a joint resolution, which requires presentment to the President" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 16 the Senate Democrats' wrap-up said that no later than Friday, October 2, the Senate would take a concurrent resolution numbered H.Con.Res. 89 out of the Foreign Relations Committee and vote on it. The House text of that resolution directs the president to remove U.S. forces from hostilities against Iran.<Cite s="senate-dems-wrapup-2026-09-16" passage="no later than Friday, October 2nd, H.Con.Res.89 will be discharged" /><Cite s="hconres-89-eh-2026-07-23" passage="from hostilities against the Islamic" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 18 the Associated Press reported Central Command's estimate: ${centcom.total} billion as of September 3, in a one-page breakdown to Congress that covers warships, planes, bases, equipment losses, and medical support for troops, with munitions at ${centcom.munitions} billion and damage to bases still left out. The same report says Democratic senators wrote to Hegseth demanding a full accounting of the war's costs. CBO's ${table1.total} billion and Central Command's ${centcom.total} billion have cutoffs {computed.daysAug1ToSep3} days apart and count different lists of costs.<Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="$43.6 billion as of Sept. 3" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="one-page breakdown to Congress this week" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="demanding that Hegseth provide a full accounting" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="does not include damage to U.S. bases in the Middle East" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">That same day CNN, citing two sources familiar with the matter, reported that the Pentagon told Congress the war had cost ${cnnPentagon.total} billion, the ${centcom.total} billion as of September 3 plus ${cnnPentagon.fuelAdd} billion in extra fuel. CNN writes that the estimate is not a full accounting of the war's cost.<Cite s="cnn-45-1b-2026-09-18" passage="two sources familiar with the matter" /><Cite s="cnn-45-1b-2026-09-18" passage="$45.1 billion" /><Cite s="cnn-45-1b-2026-09-18" passage="an additional $1.5 billion for extra fuel" /><Cite s="cnn-45-1b-2026-09-18" passage="The estimate is not a full accounting of the war’s cost" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: whether the Senate votes on H.Con.Res. 89 by October 2; whether Congress takes up the June request after November 3; whether CBO updates its estimate past August 1; whether the Defense Department answers the next information request; the inspector general's next quarterly report; and whether the department reconciles its public death count with the Washington Post's.<Cite s="senate-dems-wrapup-2026-09-16" passage="no later than Friday, October 2nd, H.Con.Res.89 will be discharged" /><Cite s="reuters-troop-deaths-2026-09-18" passage="at least 22 military fatalities" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions the records leave</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The cost of repairing or rebuilding U.S. bases. CBO says it cannot estimate that cost because the department did not provide values and host nations might pay part of a rebuild. The inspector general and Central Command likewise leave base damage out of their totals.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO is unable to estimate the cost" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="but does not include costs" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="does not include damage to U.S. bases in the Middle East" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Diplomatic operations and foreign aid, which Boyle asked about. CBO "was unable to estimate" those costs. The State Department's ${ig.stateTotalMillion} million through June 2 in the inspector general's report is that department's own figure for that period, not CBO's missing category filled in.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO was unable to estimate potential" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="incurred $113 million in costs related to the" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A ground-force scenario. Boyle asked CBO to consider the war lasting longer than four to five weeks and deploying U.S. troops on the ground. The September 15 letter does not publish a separate ground-deployment cost case.<Cite s="boyle-letter-to-cbo-2026-03-05" passage="deploying U.S. troops on the ground in Iran" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The August 13 briefing at which, The Hill reported from a congressional aide, defense officials put the cost at more than $42 billion. No public table from that briefing is in the records, and CBO's ${admin.directRelated} billion is a reading of the June request, not that briefing.<Cite s="hill-pentagon-42b-2026-09-15" passage="Defense officials provided the updated cost estimate to congressional defense committees on Aug. 13" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$42.3 billion" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>The records do not establish whether Central Command's September 3 breakdown updates the August 13 count.</p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">How many service members have died. The inspector general's table counts {ig.table4Total} as of August 26, and AP's September 18 report gives the same {centcom.deadPerDcas} from the Defense Department's casualty system. The Washington Post reported on September 18, citing five unidentified officials, at least {postDeaths.atLeast} military fatalities, {postDeaths.aboveDatabase} more than the Pentagon's public database, and citing another unidentified official that not all of the undisclosed deaths were directly tied to the conflict. Hegseth called the report "a complete LIE."<Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="Data as of August 26, 2026" /><Cite s="ap-centcom-43-6b-2026-09-18" passage="18 dead and more than 830 wounded" /><Cite s="reuters-troop-deaths-2026-09-18" passage="five unidentified officials" /><Cite s="reuters-troop-deaths-2026-09-18" passage="at least 22 military fatalities" /><Cite s="reuters-troop-deaths-2026-09-18" passage="a complete LIE" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Primary documents and coverage</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The records</h2>
        <p data-layer="proof">The main record is CBO's letter. Boyle's request, the White House campaign release, the June 24 request letter, the inspector general's report, the House and Senate vote records, the Senate hearing page, and the Congressional Research Service report on the War Powers Resolution sit with it; the coverage was read against those documents.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Estimating the Cost of Combat Operations Against Iran" /></p>
      </section>

